I usually put one blank line between paragraphs, and often put two to
mark a change in topic.

For instance, this is a break between (two very short) paragraphs.  Now
I'm going to switch to a related but different topic.


Formatting for email/USENET and print material is done somewhat
differently, but some of the conventions can still apply.  Often in
print you will see paragraphs begining with leading indentation, but no
additional vertical space between paragraphs.  When changing topic
between two paragraphs, but not enough to warrant a separate heading,
you might see a leading vspace.

In plain text media such as this, that is somewhat harder to use.
Paragraphs without vertical breaks between them are difficult to read
because of the density; computer screens are actually fairly poor for
reading purposes, sadly.  Also, leading indentation can be annoying --
especially if someone wants to reformat/rewrap the message text, as when
inclusion becomes deep.


There are reasons for varying space between sections of text, just as
there are between sections of code.  *Usually* one line between each
paragraph is sufficient, but where you move from topic to topic it's
useful to put a slightly larger gap between the paragraphs.



