It's an artifact of typewriters using fixed-width fonts, which was
understandable since it makes it easier -- possible!  -- to create
tables and the like.  It made it possible to clearly see the difference
between a non-sentence-ending period (as with "Mr." or "A. J. Crowley")
and a period that does end a sentence.

Modern typesetting does things a little differently.  Usually you'll see
the space following a non-sentence-ending period slightly smaller than
the space following a sentence-ending period.  Either one could be the
size of a 'normal' space, though.

(And in reality, interword gaps are *great* for helping layout and are
usually rubber widths, with post-sentence gaps getting a little bit more
stretch... 'normal space' is kind of meaningless when laying out
justified text).

